{"name":"Blue Shoal","hex":"#6b8c93","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"shoal","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Shallow."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A sandbank or sandbar creating a shallow.","A shallow in a body of water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/shoal"}],"entries":[{"title":"Shoal","blocks":[{"text":"A shoal is a natural submerged ridge, bank, or bar that consists of, or is covered by, sand or other unconsolidated material, and rises from the bed of a body of water close to the surface or above it, which poses a danger to navigation. Shoals are also known in oceanography, geomorphology, and geoscience, as sandbanks, sandbars, gravelbars, or bars. Two or more shoals that are either separated by shared troughs or interconnected by past or present sedimentary and hydrographic processes are referred to as a shoal complex."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/GughSandbar.JPG/330px-GughSandbar.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shoal","wikidata":"Q28337"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/WayaWayasewa.jpg/500px-WayaWayasewa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/WayaWayasewa.jpg/500px-WayaWayasewa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A tidal sandbar connecting the islands of Waya and Wayasewa of the Yasawa Islands, Fiji","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWayaWayasewa.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Nosy_Iranja_097.jpg/500px-Nosy_Iranja_097.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Nosy_Iranja_097.jpg/1280px-Nosy_Iranja_097.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Nosy_Iranja_097.jpg/500px-Nosy_Iranja_097.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sandbar between Nosy Iranja Be and Nosy Iranja Kely(Nosy Iranja, Madagascar)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANosy_Iranja_097.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/1_Amanohashidate_panorama_2014.jpg/500px-1_Amanohashidate_panorama_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/c